Fix TTR-expiry WAL persistence (issue #70; upstream bug, unfixed in v1.13 and
on master).

When persistence is enabled (-b <dir>), a job whose reservation times out
(TTR expires) has its timeout counter incremented in memory, but conn_timeout()
returned it to the ready queue with enqueue_job(..., update_store = 0), so
walwrite() was skipped. The on-disk binlog kept the job's previous record
(typically the one written by `put`), and on daemon restart prot_replay
restored the job with its timeouts and reserves counters reset.

Reserve WAL space via walresvupdate(&c->srv->wal) before touching the job and,
once the job is back in the ready queue, write the updated record with
walwrite() and walmaint(). The write happens after enqueue_job() rather than
through update_store = 1 because enqueue_job() also returns 0 when walwrite()
fails, and conn_timeout() would then bury a job already in the ready heap. If
no WAL space can be reserved the job is still requeued, as before, and only
its on-disk counters go stale.

--- a/prot.c
+++ b/prot.c
@@ -1902,12 +1902,25 @@
         if (j == c->out_job) {
             c->out_job = job_copy(c->out_job);
         }
+
+        /* We want to update the timeout count on disk, so reserve space for
+         * that. If none is available, still return the job to the ready
+         * queue; only the on-disk counters go stale. */
+        int z = walresvupdate(&c->srv->wal);
+        j->walresv += z;
 
         timeout_ct++; /* stats */
         j->r.timeout_ct++;
         int r = enqueue_job(c->srv, remove_this_reserved_job(c, j), 0, 0);
-        if (r < 1)
+        if (r < 1) {
             bury_job(c->srv, j, 0); /* out of memory, so bury it */
+        } else if (z) {
+            /* Written after the job is requeued: enqueue_job() also returns 0
+             * when walwrite() fails, which would bury a job already in the
+             * ready heap. */
+            walwrite(&c->srv->wal, j);
+            walmaint(&c->srv->wal);
+        }
         connsched(c);
     }
 
